00:30:14.509 --> 00:30:16.779
And then you also have to look at like, OK,
adjust for inflation.

00:30:16.779 --> 00:30:19.320
You have to adjust for the number of households
there are.

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:21.890
Now, there are, I think, more millennials
and boomers.

00:30:21.890 --> 00:30:25.510
But at one point, I mean, you go back five,
six years, that wasn't the case.

00:30:25.510 --> 00:30:26.820
Boomers are the largest generation.

00:30:26.820 --> 00:30:30.919
But, you know, as as that generation dies
off, you know, millennials basically are taking

00:30:30.919 --> 00:30:31.919
over.

00:30:31.919 --> 00:30:33.330
So I think there is some truth to that.

00:30:33.330 --> 00:30:35.500
There is truth to the student debt loads.

00:30:35.500 --> 00:30:37.240
There's truth to stagnant wages.

00:30:37.240 --> 00:30:38.929
Those things are happening.

00:30:38.929 --> 00:30:41.259
But I think you have to look at a much broader
picture.

00:30:41.259 --> 00:30:44.679
I think those are they're focusing on a very
small selection of millennials and not the

00:30:44.679 --> 00:30:49.350
entire if you look across all millennials,
you kind of aggregate things and look across

00:30:49.350 --> 00:30:50.350
the entire generation.

00:30:50.350 --> 00:30:53.070
I don't think it's as dire as the media makes
it out to be.

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:54.169
Speaker 1: So make of that case.

00:30:54.169 --> 00:30:59.179
I mean, how can we how do you defend empirically
the idea that millennials actually aren't

00:30:59.179 --> 00:31:00.549
aren't so poorly off?

00:31:00.549 --> 00:31:01.600
Speaker 4: Yes.

00:31:01.600 --> 00:31:07.140
So if you look at inflation adjusted per capita
per household, net worth the same.

00:31:07.140 --> 00:31:11.240
I'm using the same data source that they used
to say millennials are poor, using the same

00:31:11.240 --> 00:31:12.809
data source from the Federal Reserve.

00:31:12.809 --> 00:31:16.320
You look at those trends over time, and I
would have to say I could send you a charts

00:31:16.320 --> 00:31:19.960
and my blog post, but there's a chart which
shows like, OK, here's where millennials are.

00:31:19.960 --> 00:31:21.080
Here's where boomers were at the same age.

00:31:21.080 --> 00:31:22.980
And like the lines are lining up.

00:31:22.980 --> 00:31:24.470
It's like they're on the same trajectory.

00:31:24.470 --> 00:31:28.779
It's like people are just getting older and
they're we're gaining more wealth, like even

00:31:28.779 --> 00:31:29.779
during covid.

00:31:29.779 --> 00:31:33.830
The thing I remember, it's like millennials
added one trillion dollars in total wealth

00:31:33.830 --> 00:31:37.370
to their generation during covid, which was
obviously a very bad time financially for

00:31:37.370 --> 00:31:38.370
a lot of people.

00:31:38.370 --> 00:31:41.540
So we hear this stuff and like I'm not saying
that there are people struggling.

00:31:41.540 --> 00:31:43.110
I'm not trying to debate that.

00:31:43.110 --> 00:31:44.110
That's definitely true.

00:31:44.110 --> 00:31:48.750
And I can in the data, we see that clearly
the bottom 10 percent of the millennial generation

00:31:48.750 --> 00:31:53.639
or younger households in general are far,
far worse off than any bottom 10 percent,

00:31:53.639 --> 00:31:56.080
10 percent of the younger generations previously.

00:31:56.080 --> 00:31:57.580
And it's because of debt load.

00:31:57.580 --> 00:32:00.299
It's because of, you know, job prospects.

00:32:00.299 --> 00:32:04.110
There's a lot of reasons why this is happening,
but you can clearly see it in the data.

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:07.400
So if you're looking like, yeah, there's a
lot of people struggling and if you focus

00:32:07.400 --> 00:32:11.330
on that segment, you can write stories about
that which are legitimate at the same time

00:32:11.330 --> 00:32:15.139
to then paint that subset to the rest of everyone
else.

00:32:15.139 --> 00:32:17.040
I don't think it's completely fair.

00:32:17.040 --> 00:32:21.190
And so I'm just trying to bring a little bit
more balance and more object, more activity

00:32:21.190 --> 00:32:22.539
to this kind of discussion.

00:32:22.539 --> 00:32:26.059
Speaker 1: Now, when we talk about the other
thing is some of these terms, when we talk

00:32:26.059 --> 00:32:30.309
about wealth, are we talking about assets
or are we talking about net worth?

00:32:30.309 --> 00:32:36.279
Because in, for example, inflation could lead
to people having more assets in a nominal

00:32:36.279 --> 00:32:37.279
sense.

00:32:37.279 --> 00:32:40.159
But then if you account for liabilities, then
it's not so.

00:32:40.159 --> 00:32:42.080
So I think it's important to understand what
we're looking at.

00:32:42.080 --> 00:32:43.320
Speaker 4: Yes, we were.

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:45.860
And I apologize when I was talking about those
charts and wealth.

00:32:45.860 --> 00:32:48.009
I'm talking about net worth assets minus liabilities.

00:32:48.009 --> 00:32:53.499
And so like, yes, financial assets are higher,
but debt is also higher, not non.

00:32:53.499 --> 00:32:55.580
Mortgage debt is higher for millennials, without
a doubt.

00:32:55.580 --> 00:32:59.940
If you look at them and they're taking it
on earlier than that Gen X did and which who

00:32:59.940 --> 00:33:01.910
took it on even earlier than the boomers did.

00:33:01.910 --> 00:33:02.910
Right.

00:33:02.910 --> 00:33:06.690
So looking at those types of charts, you can
see that like, yes, we are we have more debt

00:33:06.690 --> 00:33:08.179
than than any other generation.

00:33:08.179 --> 00:33:10.870
But the same time we do have a little bit
more financial assets.

00:33:10.870 --> 00:33:14.190
So it kind of evens out on on aggregate.

00:33:14.190 --> 00:33:16.260
But I remember aggregate isn't what people
feel.

00:33:16.260 --> 00:33:17.929
People feel their personal situation.

00:33:17.929 --> 00:33:18.929
Sure.

00:33:18.929 --> 00:33:19.929
And just brush it away.

00:33:19.929 --> 00:33:23.269
But I don't think it's as much of a crisis
as the media portrays it.

00:33:23.269 --> 00:33:25.190
That's that's I think where I'm kind of getting
at.

00:33:25.190 --> 00:33:29.990
Speaker 1: Let's pick one kind of sub issue
within all this that sometimes talked about,

00:33:29.990 --> 00:33:31.870
like, for example, home ownership.

00:33:31.870 --> 00:33:37.500
And that's another one where you will often
see millennials are have a lower homeownership

00:33:37.500 --> 00:33:39.220
rate than prior generations.

00:33:39.220 --> 00:33:42.909
And then there will be people who say, well,
yeah, that's because the cost of living has

00:33:42.909 --> 00:33:44.320
gotten so.

00:33:44.320 --> 00:33:49.860
Significantly out of sync with wages, and
then you add to that the college debt, and

00:33:49.860 --> 00:33:53.700
then you also add to that that people are
just getting married later on.

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:55.909
And so there may be sort of cultural reasons.

00:33:55.909 --> 00:33:57.679
How how about an issue like that?

00:33:57.679 --> 00:34:02.260
How should we be thinking about the real significance
of homeownership for millennials?

00:34:02.260 --> 00:34:03.260
Speaker 4: Yeah.

00:34:03.260 --> 00:34:05.019
So I think you brought up a lot of the key
issues.

00:34:05.019 --> 00:34:06.500
There are people getting married later.

00:34:06.500 --> 00:34:07.539
That's a cultural reason.

00:34:07.539 --> 00:34:12.060
And usually when home ownership would happen,
like, for example, I'm thirty one and single

00:34:12.060 --> 00:34:14.060
right now, if I were to buy, I could buy a
house.

00:34:14.060 --> 00:34:17.630
But then if I end up getting married in the
next couple of years or something, I'm going

00:34:17.630 --> 00:34:18.630
to need a bigger house.

00:34:18.630 --> 00:34:20.070
So I would just pay the transaction costs
to do that.

00:34:20.070 --> 00:34:24.929
So I know I'm not going to be buying any time
soon until that part of my lifestyle is more

00:34:24.929 --> 00:34:25.929
cemented.

00:34:25.929 --> 00:34:30.830
That's the cultural reasons are true in terms
of you actually look at rent rent over income

00:34:30.830 --> 00:34:31.830
levels.

00:34:31.830 --> 00:34:33.079
Those levels haven't changed that much.

00:34:33.079 --> 00:34:35.540
You look at it after tax income or divided
by rent.

00:34:35.540 --> 00:34:37.200
You can look at that ratio.

00:34:37.200 --> 00:34:38.580
Those ratios are kind of flat.

00:34:38.580 --> 00:34:40.139
And so I did look at that in some way.

00:34:40.139 --> 00:34:43.139
So rent is not necessarily all that much higher.

00:34:43.139 --> 00:34:46.129
So I wouldn't say that, like, OK, wages were
stagnant.

00:34:46.129 --> 00:34:48.819
If you look at this analysis like five years
ago, this is very true.

00:34:48.819 --> 00:34:52.730
But since twenty, fourteen, twenty, fifteen,
things have kind of boomed and like incomes

00:34:52.730 --> 00:34:53.730
have gone up a lot.

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:56.139
And a lot of these stories that were written
in twenty thirteen, twenty, fourteen, twenty

00:34:56.139 --> 00:35:01.119
fifteen, they don't look as true now because
using the same data I have, if you go and

00:35:01.119 --> 00:35:04.350
stop the story in twenty thirteen, it looks
pretty dire for millennials.

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:07.059
I'm not going to lie, but you move four to
five years.

00:35:07.059 --> 00:35:10.700
It's like, oh things have caught up, you know,
things are starting to happen and it isn't

00:35:10.700 --> 00:35:11.700
looking as bad now.

00:35:11.700 --> 00:35:13.010
And so that's kind of the thing I see.

00:35:13.010 --> 00:35:16.819
So in terms of home ownership, yeah, there's
a lot of issues there.

00:35:16.819 --> 00:35:20.099
And obviously prices have gone up, but rates
are lower.

00:35:20.099 --> 00:35:24.250
So like the the cost of a mortgage may not
necessarily be that much higher or similar,

00:35:24.250 --> 00:35:26.040
but it's a complex topic.

00:35:26.040 --> 00:35:27.450
There's a lot of issues to unpack there.

00:35:27.450 --> 00:35:28.830
Speaker 1: So big picture.

00:35:28.830 --> 00:35:36.550
I mean, what what is the kind of defining
narrative overall about how we should understand

00:35:36.550 --> 00:35:41.540
the financial picture of millennials in the
aggregate understanding, as you say, that

00:35:41.540 --> 00:35:46.290
people people's personal experience is their
lived experience and that that's sort of what

00:35:46.290 --> 00:35:50.280
it's going to feel like at the individual
level, at the aggregate is is there not much

00:35:50.280 --> 00:35:54.849
of a story in terms of how millennials are
doing relative to prior generations financially?

00:35:54.849 --> 00:35:57.820
Speaker 4: I don't I don't think I think it
depends.

00:35:57.820 --> 00:36:01.180
When you look as I said, if you go back a
couple of years, it looks pretty bad.

00:36:01.180 --> 00:36:06.991
But now things of, you know, incomes have
gone up a bit, like assets have gone up as

00:36:06.991 --> 00:36:07.991
a result.

00:36:07.991 --> 00:36:11.470
Because of that, millennials aren't looking
as they don't look like they're struggling

00:36:11.470 --> 00:36:12.470
as much.

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:16.210
However, I think the real story to take away
here is there is a subset of millennials that

00:36:16.210 --> 00:36:18.069
is struggling and I think they need help.

00:36:18.069 --> 00:36:21.530
And so whether that's right now, the there's
debates about whether we should cancel student

00:36:21.530 --> 00:36:22.530
loan debt or.

00:36:22.530 --> 00:36:23.530
Right.

00:36:23.530 --> 00:36:24.530
We should have.

00:36:24.530 --> 00:36:25.530
Why not have a tax credit?

00:36:25.530 --> 00:36:28.069
Every American gets a ten thousand dollar
tax credit that you can use towards education.

00:36:28.069 --> 00:36:29.810
If you never use it, you lose it.

00:36:29.810 --> 00:36:31.970
If you've used it in the past, you can use
in the future.

00:36:31.970 --> 00:36:35.510
If you paid off student loan debt ten years
ago, guess what?

00:36:35.510 --> 00:36:36.510
You can use it.

00:36:36.510 --> 00:36:37.510
You can write it off that.

00:36:37.510 --> 00:36:40.700
I don't know exactly how that would work,
but something like that, where it's going

00:36:40.700 --> 00:36:44.460
to feel fair, everyone gets it technically
and it would help those people that are in

00:36:44.460 --> 00:36:45.460
debt at least somewhat.

00:36:45.460 --> 00:36:50.930
So I don't know if that's the solution, but
I think there are people that need help and

00:36:50.930 --> 00:36:53.320
painting the picture of all millennials are
struggling.

00:36:53.320 --> 00:36:57.550
I think that makes boomers look at millennials
say, oh, they're just lazy versus like, no,

00:36:57.550 --> 00:36:59.210
just some millennials are struggling.

00:36:59.210 --> 00:37:02.470
Then boomers may look at this and say, oh,
that's the real problem, we should help them.

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:06.490
I think it's it's much easier to help a subset
of people than to be like, oh, I'm not just

00:37:06.490 --> 00:37:09.390
going to do a massive wealth transfer to millennials,
which I don't think we need.

00:37:09.390 --> 00:37:11.560
I think it's like any sort of thing like that.

00:37:11.560 --> 00:37:15.369
I think it needs to be very targeted to the
people who are actually, like, in a really

00:37:15.369 --> 00:37:16.369
bad position.

00:37:16.369 --> 00:37:19.869
Speaker 1: Yeah, I mean, that's one of the
criticisms, one of the criticisms that comes

00:37:19.869 --> 00:37:25.840
up about the just the blanket cancelation
of student debt is that it wouldn't really

00:37:25.840 --> 00:37:27.559
be that progressive.

00:37:27.559 --> 00:37:29.120
And I'm not saying politically progressive.

00:37:29.120 --> 00:37:35.600
I'm saying in terms of who it's who it's hitting
because there are lots of people who have

00:37:35.600 --> 00:37:40.760
chosen to take on debt, even though even though
their financial position today is actually

00:37:40.760 --> 00:37:44.589
quite good and you're going to be hitting
them even though their ability to repay is

00:37:44.589 --> 00:37:49.440
actually significant, is that a fair criticism
of like a blanket student debt cancelation

00:37:49.440 --> 00:37:51.520
versus something like what you're talking
about?

00:37:51.520 --> 00:37:53.210
Speaker 4: Yes, I see.

00:37:53.210 --> 00:37:55.590
The issue is like, OK, personal responsibility.

00:37:55.590 --> 00:37:56.780
All I chose not to go.

00:37:56.780 --> 00:38:00.570
And you have to realize, like what is only
a third of Americans end up getting a college

00:38:00.570 --> 00:38:01.570
degree anyways.

00:38:01.570 --> 00:38:04.920
And so, like for me, I'm obviously in a bubble
where, like, most of my friends are college

00:38:04.920 --> 00:38:05.920
educated.

00:38:05.920 --> 00:38:07.190
So like to me it's like, oh, everyone has
a college degree.

00:38:07.190 --> 00:38:11.619
But remember, it's only about a third of Americans
creating a policy only for a third of Americans

00:38:11.619 --> 00:38:15.819
is something that like and it's not like Age-Based
or something like Social Security.

00:38:15.819 --> 00:38:17.790
It's it's something that's going to be hard
to pass.

00:38:17.790 --> 00:38:20.880
That's why I'm saying, like, try and come
up with something that's like fair.

00:38:20.880 --> 00:38:25.579
Maybe you come up with a policy where you
can use your these credits for a technical

00:38:25.579 --> 00:38:29.200
school, you can use it for anything, you can
use it for a coding boot camp.

00:38:29.200 --> 00:38:32.980
You know, if you expand it to not just be
college debt, I think you can get a lot more

00:38:32.980 --> 00:38:35.430
Americans in on it and it would actually help
people.

00:38:35.430 --> 00:38:37.920
So I think you need to just rethink that.

00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:41.109
And that's also one of the reasons why I like
canceling student loan debt.

00:38:41.109 --> 00:38:43.400
Well, that's that would be helpful to some
people I.

00:38:43.400 --> 00:38:46.650
A lot of people will see that and say, like,
yeah, that's I why am I paying for someone

00:38:46.650 --> 00:38:47.650
else's thing?

00:38:47.650 --> 00:38:50.920
So I think you need to kind of make it a little
bit more fair to get a lot more Buy-In from

00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:51.920
from everyone.

00:38:51.920 --> 00:38:52.920
I don't know how to do that.

00:38:52.920 --> 00:38:56.450
But yeah, this is
Speaker 1: the Andrew Yang idea was to include

00:38:56.450 --> 00:38:57.450
trade school.

00:38:57.450 --> 00:39:00.260
And that's not unique to Andrew Yang, just
his name comes to mind.

00:39:00.260 --> 00:39:03.410
I know Joe Biden has talked about that as
well, and so have others.

00:39:03.410 --> 00:39:07.610
I mean, I think the cultural issue around
the candidates, you've got the economic issue

00:39:07.610 --> 00:39:11.580
where there are some on the American political
right who say, well, it just shouldn't be

00:39:11.580 --> 00:39:15.800
done because either we can't afford it or
people haven't earned that cancelation because

00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:16.800
they chose to go to school.

00:39:16.800 --> 00:39:18.480
They knew what it cost, etc..

00:39:18.480 --> 00:39:24.790
There's also the political cultural side of
the perception that college is left wing indoctrination.

00:39:24.790 --> 00:39:28.680
And so there's some resistance to that because
there are people who politically on the right

00:39:28.680 --> 00:39:31.630
think, well, if you encourage more people
to go to college, that's actually going to

00:39:31.630 --> 00:39:33.110
be a problem politically.

00:39:33.110 --> 00:39:35.369
And that's probably another hurdle that would
have to be cleared.

00:39:35.369 --> 00:39:37.160
Speaker 4: Of course, both of those things.

00:39:37.160 --> 00:39:41.470
I think the fiscal arguments make more sense
than the indoctrination arguments.

00:39:41.470 --> 00:39:45.420
Obviously, yes, the universities are more
liberal for a host of reasons, but I don't

00:39:45.420 --> 00:39:47.760
think it necessarily is being indoctrination.

00:39:47.760 --> 00:39:49.480
I don't think it needs to be that strong now.

00:39:49.480 --> 00:39:52.510
But I do think the fiscal arguments you can
make those like, yeah, can we afford this,

00:39:52.510 --> 00:39:53.510
this and that?

00:39:53.510 --> 00:39:55.020
I think there are probably some multiplier
effect.

00:39:55.020 --> 00:39:58.930
I haven't really read all the studies on this,
but I think education is generally good because

00:39:58.930 --> 00:40:03.060
if you go to do with the joke is underwater
basket weaving, you know, that's probably

00:40:03.060 --> 00:40:07.579
not a good use of resources, but I think a
lot of a lot of educational things are, I

00:40:07.579 --> 00:40:08.720
think, generally positive for society.

00:40:08.720 --> 00:40:09.720
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:40:09.720 --> 00:40:13.750
I mean, I think when you look at the range
of the how stimulative different types of

00:40:13.750 --> 00:40:19.720
government spending is, are, I don't know,
grammatically with the right word is there.

00:40:19.720 --> 00:40:24.790
On the one hand, you've got like letting rich
people who have all of their expenses covered

00:40:24.790 --> 00:40:26.410
just put more money in the bank account.

00:40:26.410 --> 00:40:27.410
Right.

00:40:27.410 --> 00:40:28.410
That that would be on the low end.

00:40:28.410 --> 00:40:32.760
On the other side, things like food stamps
are extremely stimulative in terms of a multiplier

00:40:32.760 --> 00:40:33.760
effect.

00:40:33.760 --> 00:40:36.069
And we can imagine why the money is going
right back into local businesses.

00:40:36.069 --> 00:40:37.990
And it's increasing.

00:40:37.990 --> 00:40:40.119
It's a demand side stimulus in communities.

00:40:40.119 --> 00:40:43.950
I think education tends to be closer to the
food stamps side than the letting rich people

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:46.329
sock money away in a savings account side.

00:40:46.329 --> 00:40:50.010
Speaker 4: Yeah, I would tend to probably
agree with that.

00:40:50.010 --> 00:40:52.500
I mean, I haven't looked at the studies on
it, but yeah, I mean, I think education is

00:40:52.500 --> 00:40:56.160
probably closer to that than something that's
not really helpful.

00:40:56.160 --> 00:41:01.150
Speaker 1: Nick, while while we have you here,
because you've written and tweeted a lot about

00:41:01.150 --> 00:41:07.170
your ups and downs with cryptocurrency, and
if I recall correctly, a few months ago, you

00:41:07.170 --> 00:41:08.170
got out, right?

00:41:08.170 --> 00:41:09.460
Speaker 4: I got half out.

00:41:09.460 --> 00:41:10.880
Speaker 1: You got half out.

00:41:10.880 --> 00:41:14.730
How about what is your sense now of what we're
seeing?

00:41:14.730 --> 00:41:21.040
I recently did an interview with the CEO of
Celsius, who obviously has a vested financial

00:41:21.040 --> 00:41:22.559
interest in saying this is only the beginning.

00:41:22.559 --> 00:41:28.000
This is going to be great as someone who is
half out and you sort of have a casual interest

00:41:28.000 --> 00:41:30.619
in it would be cool if that went up what you
still have, but.

00:41:30.619 --> 00:41:32.009
